# =============================================================================
# THE PUBLIC REPO IS GENERATED, SO ITS LINE ENDINGS ARE A PROPERTY OF THE
# GENERATOR — NOT OF WHOEVER RAN IT
# =============================================================================
#
# `tools/sync_public_repo.py` copies bytes. It writes LF, because that is what
# the source files hold. Git on Windows then offers to convert them to CRLF on
# checkout, and the result is that ~20 files report as MODIFIED after every sync
# with byte-identical content.
#
# That is not cosmetic. The public repo is the one place in this project where a
# human reads a diff to decide what becomes visible to the world, and a diff
# that is 20 files of nothing is a diff nobody reads carefully. It is the same
# alarm-fatigue failure the detector rule names: a signal that fires every time
# stops being a signal. It made a batch of 112 changed paths look like a large
# release when only five files outside the demo had actually changed.
#
# So the generated tree declares its own line endings and stops depending on the
# platform that generated it. `eol=lf` on the working tree as well as the index:
# a sync run on Windows and a sync run in CI now produce the same bytes, and a
# clean tree after a sync means the sync genuinely changed nothing.
#
# This file lives in `public/overlay/` rather than in the public repo directly,
# because anything written into the public repo by hand is overwritten by the
# next sync. The overlay is how the generator ships a file it owns.
# =============================================================================

* text=auto eol=lf

# Binaries, so nothing tries to normalise them. The demo ships fonts and a
# social card; the docs ship a diagram.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.eot   binary
*.zip   binary
*.gz    binary

# `.build-stamp.json` is a SHA-256 over source bytes. It is text and it is
# small, but it is also the thing the freshness gate compares — normalising it
# is correct precisely because the hash inside it is computed over the SOURCE,
# not over this file.
*.json  text eol=lf
